WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.000
You pay for a coffee.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.025
A minute later you ask for a refund, because the order was wrong.

3
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:11.474
That evening, your bank's app lists the refund first.

4
00:00:11.474 --> 00:00:14.348
For a moment, it looks as if the café paid you.

5
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:20.795
In a program that keeps account balances, it's worse: a refund for a payment it has never seen.

6
00:00:20.795 --> 00:00:22.773
How did the order get swapped?

7
00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:24.025
Hold that question.

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.301
Here's the setup.

9
00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:29.383
The payments app sends every payment and every refund to Kafka.

10
00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:34.631
Kafka is a program that keeps logs of messages and lets other programs read them.

11
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.972
Each message is a record: a key, a value, which is the payment itself, and a few extra fields.

12
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.248
A log only ever adds at its end, so a log keeps its order.

13
00:00:45.248 --> 00:00:46.092
That's true.

14
00:00:46.092 --> 00:00:52.354
But the payments topic, Kafka's name for one stream of records, is six logs side by side.

15
00:00:52.354 --> 00:00:55.380
They're called partitions, numbered 0 to 5.

16
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:59.106
When the app sends a record, one of the six has to get it.

17
00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:05.019
And the one who chooses is the producer: the part of the Kafka library that runs inside the app.

18
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.716
So what rule should the producer use?

19
00:01:07.716 --> 00:01:11.049
The simplest is taking turns, called round robin.

20
00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:15.401
Record one to partition 0, record two to partition 1, and so on.

21
00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:17.237
The load spreads perfectly.

22
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:19.221
Now watch account 42.

23
00:01:19.221 --> 00:01:21.873
Its payment lands in partition 0.

24
00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:24.444
Its refund lands in partition 1.

25
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:28.814
Each partition has its own reader, and each reads at its own pace.

26
00:01:28.814 --> 00:01:29.955
Reader 1 is quick.

27
00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:35.149
So the balance program gets the refund first, for a payment it has never heard of.

28
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.986
That's a thank-you note for a present nobody has posted yet.

29
00:01:38.986 --> 00:01:44.145
So the point really is: taking turns spreads the records, and scatters each account.

30
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:49.380
What we want is one rule: every record of one account goes to the same partition.

31
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:56.357
And the app runs as several copies on several computers, so the rule has to work without them asking each other.

32
00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.016
A post room solves this exact problem.

33
00:01:59.016 --> 00:02:04.504
Picture a wall of pigeonholes, little open boxes where letters wait to be collected.

34
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.209
Every clerk works out the box from the account number on the envelope, with the same sum.

35
00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.665
Letters for one account land in one box, stacked in the order they came.

36
00:02:14.665 --> 00:02:16.027
No clerk asks another.

37
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:17.091
Let's map it.

38
00:02:17.091 --> 00:02:19.565
The account number is the record's key.

39
00:02:19.565 --> 00:02:21.150
The box is the partition.

40
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.893
The clerks are the copies of the app, each with a producer.

41
00:02:24.893 --> 00:02:26.732
And the sum is called a hash.

42
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.837
A hash function turns any bytes into a number.

43
00:02:29.837 --> 00:02:32.583
The same bytes always give the same number.

44
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:36.797
Let's follow account 17 through the producer's sum, in four steps.

45
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.217
One: turn the key into bytes.

46
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:41.664
The text acct-17 is seven bytes.

47
00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:45.572
Two: hash the bytes with murmur2, a fast hash function.

48
00:02:45.572 --> 00:02:48.941
Out comes a 32-bit number, and here it's negative.

49
00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:54.580
Three: clear the sign bit, the leftmost bit, which says whether a number is negative.

50
00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:59.548
A partition number can't be negative, so the producer keeps the other 31 bits.

51
00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:04.377
Four: divide by the number of partitions and keep the remainder.

52
00:03:04.377 --> 00:03:05.030
That's 2.

53
00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:10.399
So account 17 goes to partition 2, from every copy of the app, every time.

54
00:03:10.633 --> 00:03:16.805
This is the default partitioner, the code in the Java client library that picks the partition.

55
00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:18.095
And account 42?

56
00:03:18.095 --> 00:03:20.430
Its hash leaves a remainder of 3.

57
00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:26.163
Its payment and its refund both land in partition 3, in the order they were sent.

58
00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:27.369
Now a guess.

59
00:03:27.369 --> 00:03:31.213
acct-18 differs from acct-17 by one character.

60
00:03:31.213 --> 00:03:33.803
Will its hash be close to 17's?

61
00:03:33.803 --> 00:03:35.140
Pause and guess.

62
00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:39.304
Not even close.

63
00:03:39.304 --> 00:03:41.246
A completely different number.

64
00:03:41.246 --> 00:03:45.777
That's the job of a good hash: keys that look alike spread out evenly.

65
00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:48.825
And it still lands in partition 2, by luck.

66
00:03:48.825 --> 00:03:53.212
With six partitions, any two keys share one about one time in six.

67
00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:56.230
Here's a trap many of us would walk into.

68
00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:57.800
Step three looks fussy.

69
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.029
Why not make the number positive the usual way, with Math.abs?

70
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.346
Let's try it.

71
00:04:03.346 --> 00:04:08.372
Math.abs of that negative number, divided by 6, leaves 0.

72
00:04:08.372 --> 00:04:09.430
Partition 0.

73
00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.635
The real producer said 2.

74
00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:16.029
Clearing the sign bit isn't the same as dropping the minus sign.

75
00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:21.340
So a test that predicts partitions with Math.abs gets most negative hashes wrong.

76
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:22.030
Well...

77
00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.219
that's one very confident clerk, standing at the wrong box.

78
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.652
Two warnings.

79
00:04:27.652 --> 00:04:32.348
First, the sum lives in each client library, not in Kafka.

80
00:04:32.348 --> 00:04:42.791
Programs in Python or Go often use librdkafka, a library written in C, and by default it hashes with a different function, CRC32.

81
00:04:42.967 --> 00:04:45.302
That's a clerk with a different sum.

82
00:04:45.302 --> 00:04:50.425
Same key, maybe a different box, unless that program is set to use murmur2 too.

83
00:04:50.633 --> 00:04:54.420
Second, the sum divides by the number of partitions.

84
00:04:54.420 --> 00:05:00.026
Grow the topic from 6 to 8, and acct-1 moves from partition 2 to partition 6.

85
00:05:00.026 --> 00:05:02.429
Its old records stay behind in 2.

86
00:05:02.633 --> 00:05:04.573
About three keys in four move.

87
00:05:04.573 --> 00:05:08.065
So choose the partition count once, with room to grow.

88
00:05:08.267 --> 00:05:11.215
In Java and Kotlin, all you choose is the key.

89
00:05:11.215 --> 00:05:16.728
Pass the account id as the record's key, and the producer does the four steps for you.

90
00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:20.410
So how did the refund get ahead of its payment?

91
00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:24.857
They went into two different logs, and nothing keeps order across logs.

92
00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:30.244
One key per account puts both in one partition, and inside one partition, order holds.

93
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.463
Same key, same sum, same box.

94
00:05:32.463 --> 00:05:35.036
Choose the key for the order you need.

95
00:05:35.633 --> 00:05:36.827
One quick check.

96
00:05:36.827 --> 00:05:39.439
card-1 hashes to a positive number.

97
00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:44.215
Would a test that uses Math.abs pick the right partition for it?

98
00:05:44.215 --> 00:05:45.409
Pause and think.

99
00:05:48.633 --> 00:05:48.950
Yes.

100
00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:54.815
For a positive number, Math.abs changes nothing, so both give partition 5.

101
00:05:54.815 --> 00:05:58.381
It only goes wrong when the hash is negative.
